WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.002 --> 00:00:11.591
[The Universe in Verse]

00:00:12.717 --> 00:00:13.885
[Prelude]

00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:16.221
In 1908,

00:00:16.263 --> 00:00:17.889
Henrietta Swan Leavitt,

00:00:17.931 --> 00:00:20.475
one of the women known
as the Harvard Computers,

00:00:20.517 --> 00:00:23.019
who changed our understanding
of the universe

00:00:23.061 --> 00:00:25.230
long before they could vote,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.693
was cataloging photographic plates
at the Harvard College Observatory,

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:34.239
single-handedly analyzing
2,000 variable stars --

00:00:34.281 --> 00:00:35.991
stars with fluctuating brightness,

00:00:36.032 --> 00:00:38.326
kind of like a lighthouse beacon --

00:00:38.326 --> 00:00:41.121
when she began noticing
a distinct correlation

00:00:41.121 --> 00:00:44.457
between their brightness
and their blinking pattern.

00:00:44.457 --> 00:00:48.169
That correlation made it possible
to measure the distance of stars

00:00:48.211 --> 00:00:49.796
for the very first time,

00:00:49.838 --> 00:00:52.716
furnishing the yardstick of the universe.

00:00:53.341 --> 00:00:57.178
Meanwhile, a dutiful
teenage boy in the Midwest

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.098
was repressing his childhood
love of astronomy

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.185
and beginning his legal studies
to fulfill his dying father's demand

00:01:04.227 --> 00:01:07.105
for an ordinary, reputable life.

00:01:07.606 --> 00:01:09.608
When his father died,

00:01:09.608 --> 00:01:13.903
Edwin Hubble turned that passion
for the stars into formal study

00:01:13.945 --> 00:01:18.158
and discovered two revolutionary
facts about the universe:

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.078
that it is tremendously
bigger than we thought

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.123
and that it's getting bigger
and bigger by the blink.

00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:28.543
One October evening in 1923,

00:01:28.585 --> 00:01:31.921
perched at the foot
of the largest telescope in the world,

00:01:31.921 --> 00:01:35.050
at Mount Wilson Observatory,
not far from here,

00:01:35.091 --> 00:01:38.803
Hubble took a 45-minute
exposure of Andromeda,

00:01:38.845 --> 00:01:43.224
which was then thought to be one
of many spiral nebulae in our Milky Way.

00:01:43.808 --> 00:01:45.226
The notion of a galaxy,

00:01:45.268 --> 00:01:50.482
a gravitationally bound cluster
of stars and dust and dark matter,

00:01:50.482 --> 00:01:52.317
didn't exist at the time.

00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:55.403
The Milky Way, which, by the way,
was coined as a term

00:01:55.445 --> 00:01:57.906
by Chaucer in the 14th century,

00:01:57.906 --> 00:02:01.326
was thought to be
what was called an "island universe"

00:02:01.368 --> 00:02:06.081
beyond the edge of which lay
cold, dark nothingness.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.085
But when Hubble looked
at the photograph the next morning

00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:12.629
and compared it to previous ones,

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:14.381
he furrowed his brow

00:02:14.381 --> 00:02:19.844
and with a gasp of revelation
scribbled right on top of the plate "VAR"

00:02:19.844 --> 00:02:22.138
and then drew an exclamation point

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.309
because he had realized
that a tiny fleck in Andromeda

00:02:26.351 --> 00:02:29.020
previously thought to be a nova

00:02:29.062 --> 00:02:30.980
could not possibly be a nova,

00:02:31.022 --> 00:02:34.275
given its blinking pattern
across these different photographs.

00:02:34.609 --> 00:02:37.737
And it was, in fact, a variable star.

00:02:38.113 --> 00:02:41.408
Which meant, given
Henrietta Leavitt's data,

00:02:41.449 --> 00:02:44.035
that it was very, very far away,

00:02:44.035 --> 00:02:46.996
farther than the edge of the Milky Way.

00:02:47.414 --> 00:02:52.794
Which meant that Andromeda
was not a nebula in our own galaxy,

00:02:52.836 --> 00:02:55.255
but a whole other galaxy,

00:02:55.255 --> 00:02:58.591
out there, in the cold, dark nothingness.

00:02:59.008 --> 00:03:02.554
Suddenly, the universe was a garden,
blooming with galaxies,

00:03:02.595 --> 00:03:05.098
with ours but a single bloom.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.685
That same year, in another country,

00:03:08.727 --> 00:03:10.937
suspended between two World Wars,

00:03:10.979 --> 00:03:15.692
another young scientist
came up with the radical idea

00:03:15.692 --> 00:03:18.069
of using rocket technology,

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:20.739
this deadly military technology,

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:25.160
to mount an enormous telescope
and launch it into Earth's orbit.

00:03:25.201 --> 00:03:28.413
Not only closer to the stars,
but bypassing the atmosphere

00:03:28.413 --> 00:03:31.082
that occludes our terrestrial instruments.

00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:34.544
It would take another
two generations of scientists

00:03:34.586 --> 00:03:37.589
to make that telescope a reality:

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:42.010
a shimmering poem of metal,
physics and perseverance

00:03:42.051 --> 00:03:43.720
bearing Hubble's name.

00:03:44.512 --> 00:03:48.183
But when the Hubble Space Telescope
launched in 1990,

00:03:48.224 --> 00:03:52.854
our human fallibility had slipped
into the precision of the instrument,

00:03:52.896 --> 00:03:56.065
and it turned out that its primary mirror

00:03:56.107 --> 00:03:59.152
was ground into the wrong spherical shape,

00:03:59.194 --> 00:04:03.615
warping these long-awaited images
of the unfathomed universe.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.869
Up the coast from
Mount Wilson Observatory,

00:04:07.911 --> 00:04:12.040
a teenage girl watched her father,
who had worked on the Hubble,

00:04:12.081 --> 00:04:16.419
one of NASA's first Black engineers,
come home brokenhearted.

00:04:16.878 --> 00:04:19.672
He didn't know that his observant daughter

00:04:19.714 --> 00:04:22.801
would become poet laureate of his country

00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:26.179
and would commemorate him
in the tenderest tribute

00:04:26.179 --> 00:04:30.475
that an artist's daughter
has ever composed for a scientist father,

00:04:30.475 --> 00:04:32.352
which would win her the Pulitzer Prize

00:04:32.393 --> 00:04:36.898
the year the Hubble's corrected optics
captured that revolutionary,

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.902
ultra-deep field image
of the observable universe,

00:04:40.944 --> 00:04:43.029
revealing what neither Hubble

00:04:43.071 --> 00:04:45.990
nor Henrietta Leavitt could have imagined.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:50.662
That ours is not just one
of several galaxies,

00:04:50.662 --> 00:04:55.375
but that there are 100 billion
galaxies out there,

00:04:55.375 --> 00:05:00.129
each containing 100 billion stars.

00:05:01.214 --> 00:05:05.218
Narrator: From “My God,
It’s Full of Stars.”

00:05:08.012 --> 00:05:12.684
When my father worked
on the Hubble Telescope, he said

00:05:12.725 --> 00:05:18.022
They operated like surgeons:
scrubbed and sheathed

00:05:18.064 --> 00:05:24.404
In papery green,
the room a clean, cold and bright white.

00:05:25.321 --> 00:05:29.993
He’d read Larry Niven at home,
and drink scotch on the rocks,

00:05:30.034 --> 00:05:36.249
His eyes exhausted and pink.
These were the Reagan years,

00:05:36.249 --> 00:05:40.795
When we lived with our
finger on The Button and struggled

00:05:40.837 --> 00:05:47.719
To view our enemies as children.
My father spent whole seasons

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.182
Bowing before the oracle-eye,
hungry for what it would find.

00:05:53.892 --> 00:06:00.189
His face lit-up whenever anyone asked,
and his arms would rise

00:06:00.231 --> 00:06:05.361
As if he were weightless,
perfectly at ease in the never-ending

00:06:05.445 --> 00:06:12.368
Night of space. On the ground, 
we tied postcards to balloons

00:06:12.702 --> 00:06:18.416
For peace. Prince Charles married Lady Di.

00:06:18.499 --> 00:06:20.418
Rock Hudson died.

00:06:22.128 --> 00:06:28.092
We learned new words for things.
The decade changed.

00:06:29.302 --> 00:06:35.183
The first few pictures came back blurred,
and I felt ashamed

00:06:35.224 --> 00:06:42.106
For all the cheerful engineers,
my father and his tribe. The second time,

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:47.654
The optics jibed.
We saw to the edge of all there is --

00:06:48.696 --> 00:06:54.827
So brutal and alive
it seemed to comprehend us back.

